      "math_academy_id": "q-333625",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-1}} is {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=-3x$ is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-3x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14122747/assets/f20c6478af528b45ab2ea7dfded3e835c49a79178e11d7dd06bb13760874bbdb.png)",
